\details{
The \code{robots_product_token} is used ONLY for robots user-agent group
selection; it is never sent as an HTTP header. Each matcher backend is
authoritative only for its own vendor semantics: \code{google} for Google,
\code{yandex} for Yandex, \code{bing} for Bing, and RFC 9309 behavior only under the
\code{rfc9309} backend. The Google backend additionally accepts any valid Google
robots product token and returns Google parsing/matching semantics for it.
The Yandex and Bing backends are bounded to their supported vendor profiles
only and never generalize to arbitrary tokens. This boundary is published
as \code{robots_engine_contract_v1()$matcher_capability}.

Under the \code{google} ruleset with the \code{google} backend, the result is also a
crawler-aware layer above the matcher for the Google crawler tokens
published as \code{matcher_capability$google$crawler_tokens} (schema revision
\code{2026-11-12.1}). A crawler that Google's common-crawlers page lists with a
parent token (\code{Googlebot-Image}, \code{Googlebot-Video}, \code{Googlebot-News},
\code{Google-InspectionTool} and \code{Google-CloudVertexBot} list \code{Googlebot};
\code{GoogleOther-Image} and \code{GoogleOther-Video} list \code{GoogleOther}) obeys the
groups naming its own token, else those naming its parent token, else
\code{*}; the two are never merged. \code{AdsBot-Google}, \code{AdsBot-Google-Mobile},
\code{Mediapartners-Google} and \code{APIs-Google} ignore \code{*}, so with no group of
their own they are allowed, with no line. The token is compared whole and
ASCII case-insensitively (\code{Googlebot-Image/1.0} is not listed). For these
tokens the result follows what Google documents for its crawlers; it is
read from Google's documentation, not observed from the crawlers. Under
every other ruleset, and for every other token, it is Google's matching
for the token, NOT a claim of compatibility with, or a prediction of, the
crawler the token names. The \code{group_token} column says whose group
decided a Google row: the token as given, its parent token, \code{"*"} when no
group names it (the \code{*} rules, if any), or \code{NA} for a \code{*}-ignoring
crawler with no group of its own, a row of another backend, or a row no
matcher decided. \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_text]{allowed_by_robots_text()}} and \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_url]{allowed_by_robots_url()}}
apply none of this layer.

The Yandex backend is active (since schema revision \code{2026-07-18.2}). An
evaluated Yandex row publishes \code{reason} as one of \code{default_allow},
\code{rule_allow}, \code{rule_disallow}, or \code{effective_empty_disallow}; a non-evaluated
Yandex row reports \code{matcher_status = "not_evaluated"} with
\code{reason = "unsupported_product_token"}
(\code{error_class = "robots_unsupported_product_token"}) or
\code{reason = "invalid_request_target"}
(\code{error_class = "robots_invalid_request_target"}) at
\code{error_stage = "input"}. The activation adds the public
\code{matched_rule_value_raw} list column (one element per row: exact owning-rule
bytes, \code{raw(0)} for an effective-empty Disallow, or \code{NULL} for an absent
rule); it is populated for Yandex-evaluated rows and \code{NULL} elsewhere. The
vendored-matcher identity is published as
\code{robots_engine_contract_v1()$matcher_identity$yandex}.

A Google row reports no line for a rule with an empty value: when the
selected rule is an \verb{Allow:} or \verb{Disallow:} with nothing after the colon,
the row reports \code{url_decision = "allow"} with \code{reason = "default_allow"},
\code{matched_rule_type = "none"}, \code{NA} \code{matched_line} and \code{matched_rule_value},
and a \code{NULL} \code{matched_rule_value_raw}; the legacy functions report the
same decision source, type, line and value (\code{design/PRD.md} section 6.6,
"Match metadata rules"). When a Yandex row's selected rule is an empty
\code{Disallow}, it instead reports \code{effective_empty_disallow} with that line.

The Google backend reads the first 512,000 bytes of a body (Google's
documented 500 KiB limit) and matches their complete lines: a rule after
byte 512,000, or a line that byte splits, is not read. It looks one byte
past the window, so a line whose line break is byte 512,001 is kept. A
row over the window has \code{matcher_body_truncated = TRUE}, and
\code{matcher_input_bytes} counts the bytes matched. \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_text]{allowed_by_robots_text()}}
has no window and reads the whole body; \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_url]{allowed_by_robots_url()}} reads
the complete lines of its \code{max_bytes} cut, 512,000 bytes by default
(\code{design/engine-profiles.md} section 7).

Since schema revision \code{2026-11-21.1}, \code{url_normalization} decides which
URL the matcher reads (\code{design/adr/0002-evaluator-url-preprocessing.md}).
Under \code{"rfc3986"}, the default, the URL is split the RFC 3986 way and
rebuilt as scheme, \verb{//}, host and port, the path with its dot segments
removed (section 5.2.4), and the query: userinfo is left out, so Google no
longer reads \verb{https://u;v@host/x} from its \verb{;}, and the fragment is
dropped. Only the literal segments \code{.} and \code{..} are removed; no byte is
percent-encoded, decoded or case-folded, so \verb{/\%2e\%2e/} is kept. A URL that
is not a valid RFC 3986 URI (a raw space, a byte outside ASCII, a
backslash, another character RFC 3986 excludes, or a \verb{\%} without two hex
digits) is still evaluated. A URL with no authority, such as \code{"/a/../b"},
keeps the bytes before its first \code{/} and has its dot segments removed from
there on, unless that would start the path with \verb{//} and so read as a
host (\code{"/a/..//h/x"}): then it is evaluated as given. Under \code{"none"} the
matcher reads the URL as given, as before. The \code{url} column always holds
the input and \code{matcher_url} the URL the matcher read.
\code{url_preprocessing_note} is \code{NA} under \code{"none"}, and under \code{"rfc3986"}
when the input is a valid RFC 3986 URI with an authority and the
preprocessing changed nothing. Otherwise it says why the input is not a
valid URI, that it has no authority, and which rule changed it (or why
its dot segments were left in place), joined by \code{"; "}.
\code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_text]{allowed_by_robots_text()}} and \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_url]{allowed_by_robots_url()}} read the URL as
given.

Since schema revision \code{2026-11-21.2}, \code{"whatwg"} reads the URL as the
WHATWG URL Standard serializes it (through \code{rurl::serialize_url()}),
which models browsers and Node \code{fetch}. It removes literal and encoded
dot segments (\verb{/\%2e\%2e/} too) and, unlike \code{"rfc3986"}, changes the
encoding and host as that standard does (a space becomes \verb{\%20}, a byte
outside ASCII is percent-encoded, the host is put in lower case and a
default port dropped). Userinfo is left out and the fragment dropped, as
under \code{"rfc3986"}. The URL's bytes are read as UTF-8 whatever the
session's locale (a \code{"latin1"}-marked URL is converted from latin1). A
URL with no scheme, such as \code{"/a/../b"} or \code{"example.com/a/./b"}, is read
from its first \code{/} as under \code{"rfc3986"}: that path is appended to the
placeholder origin \verb{http://h.invalid} and serialized, and the matcher
reads the bytes before the \code{/} followed by the serialized path and query
(\code{"/b"}, \code{"example.com/a/b"}); one with no \code{/} is read as under
\code{"rfc3986"}. When the serialized path starts with \verb{//}
(\code{"/a/..//h/x"}), the matcher reads \verb{http://h.invalid//h/x}, so the path
stays \verb{//h/x} as a browser would request it. A URL it cannot parse is
evaluated as given, with the note
\verb{not a valid WHATWG URL: evaluated as given}; a changed URL has the note
\verb{WHATWG URL serialization changed the URL}, then \verb{userinfo removed} or
\verb{fragment dropped} where those apply. \code{"whatwg"} serializes a URL of any
length, and its cost grows faster than linearly on a long path with many
dot segments (in \code{rurl::serialize_url()}).

In every mode, \code{preprocessing_disagreement} compares the URL the matcher
reads under \code{"rfc3986"} with the one it reads under \code{"whatwg"}, on the
rows the matcher decided (\code{url_decision} not \code{NA}); the \code{"whatwg"} form
is computed only for those rows. It is \code{TRUE} when the two differ and
are decided differently, or when \code{"whatwg"} cannot parse the URL, and
\code{FALSE} otherwise. Any other row (an \code{NA} or empty URL, an empty token,
\code{capability_unavailable}, a policy decision, a token or target the
backend refuses) is always \code{FALSE}, with \code{NA} \code{disagreement_clients}.
The second match runs only on rows whose two URLs differ in more than
the scheme or host case, a default port, an empty path against \code{/}, or
the fragment. \code{disagreement_clients} is \code{NA} when the flag is \code{FALSE};
otherwise it gives each side's decision. When the two forms differ only
through encoded dot segments (\verb{\%2e}, \verb{.\%2e}, \verb{\%2e.} or \verb{\%2e\%2e} as a
whole segment, either case), it also names the clients surveyed to
handle dot segments that way:
\verb{dot segments as in: wget 1.25, curl/libcurl < 8.13.0} for \code{"rfc3986"}
and \verb{dot segments as in: Chrome 157, Node 26 fetch, curl/libcurl >= 8.13.0} for \code{"whatwg"}. Otherwise it says the forms differ in more than
dot segments and that no client survey covers this, or, when \code{"whatwg"}
cannot parse the URL, gives the \code{"rfc3986"} decision and says so. Under
\code{"rfc3986"} and \code{"none"}, a decided row
whose URL is longer than 2048 bytes is not compared: the \code{"whatwg"} form
is not computed, no second match runs, \code{preprocessing_disagreement} is
\code{NA} and \code{disagreement_clients} is
\verb{not compared: URL longer than 2048 bytes}. Under \code{"whatwg"} every length
is compared.
}
